aic_btusb: free dropped skbs and stop the reader spinning on OOM

Two defects in the vendor AIC8800 BT-over-USB transport that together turn a
sustained HCI RX burst into an unrecoverable box hang.  Both are present in
every AIC drop we have seen, including the newest (RELEASE_DATE
2026_0123_5f7be68d, == radxa-pkg/aic8800 5.0+git20260123.5f7be68d-7), so
upgrading the driver alone does not fix them.

Context: aic_btusb does not use the kernel Bluetooth stack.  It keeps a
private 500-entry ring (aic_skb_queue) filled from URB completion via
hci_recv_frame() -> hci_send_to_stack(), and drained by a chardev read().

1) aic_enqueue() leaks every dropped skb.
   hci_send_to_stack() hands over a fresh pskb_copy() and never looks at it
   again; hci_recv_frame() frees only the *original*.  When the ring is full
   aic_enqueue() logs "Queue is full, entry will be dropped" and returns
   without freeing, so the copy is leaked permanently -- one skb per packet,
   for as long as the burst lasts.

   The vendor's only backpressure (bypass_event()) drops BLE *advertising
   reports* once the queue passes QUEUE_SIZE-100.  HID input reports arrive
   as ACL data, not advertising reports, so a held key on a BT-HID keyboard
   bypasses that guard entirely.

   Fixed by freeing the dropped skb.  The free is deferred until after
   spin_unlock_irqrestore() because aic_enqueue() runs from URB completion
   with interrupts disabled, and the warning is rate-limited -- at flood rate
   the unthrottled printk is itself a denial of service.

2) btchr_read() spins at 100% CPU when aic_dequeue_try() fails.
   On a partial read (deq_len < skb->len) aic_dequeue_try() returns
   pskb_copy(..., GFP_ATOMIC), which can fail under memory pressure.  It then
   returns NULL while leaving the entry queued.  btchr_read()'s
   wait_event_interruptible(!is_queue_empty()) is still satisfied, so the
   while (count) loop never sleeps and never exits -- a hard livelock in the
   syscall, reached exactly when memory is already short.

   Fixed by returning -ENOMEM instead of looping, letting userspace retry.

Defect 1 exhausts memory; that makes defect 2 fire; the result is an
unkillable spinning reader on an OOM box.  This is consistent with the
observed failure (deck hard-hangs on a held BT keyboard key, SSH dies with
it, watchdog reset ~16 s, no kernel trace) but that end-to-end link is
inferred, not yet proven on hardware -- it needs a board run to confirm.

Applies to: kernel/src/na-allwinner-bsp-618 (BSP graft tree), against the
newer aic8800 drop carried on branches linux-6.18.x / linux-6.18.y.
NOTE: the copy on our current branch (linux-6.18.z) is CRLF; the .x/.y copy
is LF-normalised.  This patch targets the LF copy.

Not-yet-tested-on: hardware (board offline at time of writing)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/aic8800/usb/aic_btusb/aic_btusb.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/aic8800/usb/aic_btusb/aic_btusb.c
@@ -29,6 +29,7 @@
 #include <linux/sched.h>
 #include <linux/errno.h>
 #include <linux/skbuff.h>
+#include <linux/netdevice.h>
 #include <linux/usb.h>
 
 #include <linux/ioctl.h>
@@ -698,18 +699,32 @@
 static struct sk_buff *aic_skb_queue[QUEUE_SIZE];
 static int aic_skb_queue_front = 0;
 static int aic_skb_queue_rear = 0;
+static unsigned long aic_skb_queue_drop_cnt = 0;
+static unsigned long aic_skb_queue_drop_warn_j = 0;
 
 static void aic_enqueue(struct sk_buff *skb)
 {
 	unsigned long flags = 0;
 	
+	bool dropped = false;
+	unsigned long dropped_total = 0;
+
 	spin_lock_irqsave(&queue_lock, flags);
     if (aic_skb_queue_front == (aic_skb_queue_rear + 1) % QUEUE_SIZE) {
         /*
          * If queue is full, current solution is to drop
          * the following entries.
+         *
+         * The caller hands ownership of skb to us unconditionally (every
+         * call site passes a fresh pskb_copy()), so a dropped entry must be
+         * freed or it is leaked forever.  A sustained RX burst that outruns
+         * the chardev reader -- e.g. a held key on a BT-HID keyboard, or an
+         * advertising storm -- otherwise leaks one skb per packet until the
+         * machine dies.  Note the free is deferred to outside the lock: we
+         * are called from URB completion with interrupts disabled here.
          */
-        AICBT_WARN("%s: Queue is full, entry will be dropped", __func__);
+        dropped = true;
+        dropped_total = ++aic_skb_queue_drop_cnt;
     } else {
         aic_skb_queue[aic_skb_queue_rear] = skb;
 
@@ -718,6 +733,17 @@
 
     }
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&queue_lock, flags);
+
+	if (dropped) {
+		dev_kfree_skb_any(skb);
+		/*
+		 * Rate-limit the warning: at flood rate an unthrottled printk is
+		 * itself a denial of service.
+		 */
+		if (printk_timed_ratelimit(&aic_skb_queue_drop_warn_j, 5000))
+			AICBT_WARN("%s: Queue is full, entry dropped (total %lu)",
+			           __func__, dropped_total);
+	}
 }
 
 static struct sk_buff *aic_dequeue_try(unsigned int deq_len)
@@ -1285,6 +1311,20 @@
             kfree_skb(skb);
             break;
         }
+
+        /*
+         * aic_dequeue_try() returned NULL with a non-empty queue.  That
+         * means its partial-read pskb_copy(GFP_ATOMIC) failed, and since
+         * the wait_event condition (!is_queue_empty()) is still true this
+         * loop would spin at 100% CPU in the syscall forever -- precisely
+         * when the box is already short on memory.  Bail out instead and
+         * let userspace retry.
+         */
+        if (!is_queue_empty()) {
+            AICBT_ERR("%s: dequeue failed on non-empty queue, -ENOMEM", __func__);
+            ret = -ENOMEM;
+            break;
+        }
     }
 
     return ret;
